  "response": "The dispute began with the material itself. The hominin remains were excavated from Liang Bua cave on the Indonesian island of Flores in 2003, and the unexpected anatomy of that skeleton led directly to the Liang Bua fossils being assigned to a newly erected species called Homo floresiensis; in other words, researchers gave the Flores remains a novel species designation, Homo floresiensis. One cranial figure framed everything that followed: the endocranial volume of LB1, the type specimen, was estimated at roughly 400 cubic centimetres.\n\nThat remarkably low estimate is what drove the pathological reading of the find, for it caused Teuku Jacob to argue that LB1 was a microcephalic modern human from a local pygmoid population. Subsequently, a 2014 paper attributed LB1's anatomy to Down syndrome, and that diagnosis produced a revised reading of the braincase, so that the endocranial volume of the specimen was measured at 1,100 cubic centimetres. The pathology camp therefore rested on several distinct clinical diagnoses, and choosing among them demanded quantitative comparison rather than clinical intuition.\n\nThe decisive test came from the braincase. Dean Falk's team generated a virtual endocast of LB1 from computed tomography scans; as a direct result of that endocast, the comparative endocast study published in Science concluded that the brain shape of LB1 differed from that of microcephalic modern humans. Because that result turned on the configuration of the endocast rather than on cranial capacity alone, it could not be deflected by appeals to the sheer smallness of the specimen's skull, and it shifted the burden of proof onto those who favoured a disease model.\n\nThe postcranial skeleton pointed in the same direction. The LB1 wrist bones retain a primitive morphology resembling that of early hominins and African apes, which indicates that the Flores hominins retained primitive skeletal traits absent in modern humans. Such features are not produced by any known growth disorder, and so, on any coherent reading of the skeleton, at least one of two conclusions must hold, and perhaps both of them do: that these hominins retained primitive skeletal traits absent in modern humans, or that their small body size arose through evolutionary dwarfing under island conditions.\n\nThe deeper stratigraphic record of Flores supplied the final element of the adjudication. Hominin fossils with small body size were recovered at the Mata Menge site on Flores in deposits about 700,000 years old. The Flores hominin population survived on the island until about 12,000 years ago. The combined weight of the endocast comparisons, the primitive wrist and shoulder anatomy, and the older Flores material thus resolved the controversy in favour of a genuinely distinct small-bodied hominin, leaving the pathological diagnoses without observational support.",
